Wake the ports the kernel is not waking, and say which ones are being watched.

The port report added last round answered the question it was built for, and
the answer was the one that needed the most work. With every daemon running
again, lsd finally has mail -- one message queued on one of its ports -- and
lsd is asleep in its run loop with three worker threads parked in
__workq_kernreturn. The message is there, somebody is listening for it, and
nobody was told.

That is a lost wakeup, and it is not lsd's lost wakeup in particular. Every
XPC server takes its requests through a dispatch source on a mach port, so
every synchronous XPC call in this system has the same shape: the sender
blocks in _dispatch_mach_send_and_wait_for_reply, which cannot fail -- its
default branch is "cbnz w0, brk #1" -- and the receiver never runs. It is why
SpringBoard has hung in the same instruction on every boot for weeks, and why
a ten second timeout, when it was tried, fired in syslogd, misd, installd,
backboardd, routined, timed, awdd and lsd within half a minute.

Two things go in.

The report gains one word per port: WATCHED when a kqueue has a knote on it,
unwatched otherwise. That separates "nobody is listening" from "somebody is
listening and was never told", which are the same line of output today and
want opposite repairs.

ipc_mqueue_kick_all() walks every task's receive rights and re-fires the
knote on each port that is holding mail, under exactly the conditions
ipc_mqueue_post checks before it wakes one: an active port, a receiver name,
a live receiving space, a usable klist, and somebody on it. The shim calls it
on a timer, every kickms milliseconds, and kickms defaults to zero so nothing
changes unless it is asked for.

It is a workaround and not a fix: the latency it leaves is the interval, and
the real defect -- whatever is swallowing the edge between ipc_mqueue_post
and the workqueue thread request -- is still there. But a system that answers
late answers. The point of this round is to learn whether the missing wakeup
is the whole story, and if it is, to see the screen while the cause is still
being looked for.
